solation enforcement rule, not a graph dependency. The change is structural: rewrite the persona invocation harness to strip the brief's constraints and incidents before dispatch.", 'effort_risk': 'Low. The harness already separates brief parsing from persona context assembly. Remove four string assignments; add a validation gate that rejects any persona context not sourced from committed state. Risk: none if gate is deterministic stdlib.', 'rationale': "FR-893 and FR-890 D-9 show that author framing in the brief contaminates convergence signal and enables echo-as-discovery. Removing the brief's constraints and incidents sections from persona context eliminates the seeding mechanism entirely. Personas then converge only on repository-committed facts, making convergence genuine independent discovery."}, {'persona': 'librarian', 'candidate': 'Registered Reports embed pre-specification into peer review: authors submit introduction and methods—including pre-registered hypotheses—before data collection, preventing HARKing and p-hacking by locking analysis plans before outcomes are known.', 'solution_class': 'external-method', 'verdict': 'pursue', 'precedent': 'https://www.researchgate.net/publication/343080321_Pre-registration_and_registered_reports', 'is_this_a_graph': 'No. Registered Reports is a peer-review workflow, not a graph structure. It enforces temporal ordering (specification before data) and role separation (reviewers see methods before results).', 'effort_risk': 'Medium. Requires upfront specification discipline and review-gate enforcement, but uses standard academic infrastructure. Risk: authors may resist pre-commitment; mitigation is clear incentive alignment.', 'rationale': "FR-890's core failure is that author-supplied context (brief constraints) contaminates persona independence, producing echo-chamber convergence. Registered Reports solve this by enforcing specification closure before observation—personas would commit analysis plans before seeing any 